JH wrote:
> 
> Seriously though, light only enchances a few cases.  Large dark areas are 
> nice to have light in, but the mines are about the only source of such 
> areas.  In my game, all of them were lit (IIRC), and even if they weren't a 
> regular oil lamp (or my two) would have been sufficient.  Once I'm in 
> Gehennom, the maziness makes it such that seeing an extra square in a couple 
> directions doesn't really seem that useful to me.  To each their own.

It's not an extra square, it's (pl.) extra squares. Skipping the argument of
foresight seeing monsters, you forgot (or don't value high) that you not only
save the boredom of exploring every path but will also let you significantly
reduce the amount of "discovering" traps the unexpected way (which may or may
not be of concern in some situation). For the top-down discovery of Gehennom
it's quite supporting. Also in the deeper Dungeons of Doom before the castle
light is important since you often don't have MR at that point and risk to
ruin your armor in polytraps when you explore the dark parts of the level;
again, with light you won't avoid that but you would significantly reduce the
probability to hit a trap. Adding all levels where light is useful sums up to
Mines, deeper Dungeons of Doom, Gehennom, and Astral, which is quite most of
the dungeon. Thus having a single permanent tool for all time is a Good Thing.
Most things in Nethack can be compensated, in this case by large amounts of
magic mapping (which I usually don't have in large amounts; or the spell which
I also usually don't have or don't know to cast), and/or a couple of ordinary
lamps (which I might have or not), and/or monster detection (potions are heavy
and too few) or ESP (only functional in some cases; had a problem with a green
slime recently).

But to each their own, as you said.

